      "math_academy_id": "e-14639",
      "problem": "Find a fraction that is equivalent to $\\dfrac {15}{2\\sqrt{5}}$ that does not contain a radical in the denominator.",
      "worked_solution": "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{5}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{5}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{2\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{15\\sqrt{5}}{2(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{15\\sqrt{5}}{2\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{15\\sqrt{5}}{10} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
